# tier: original-diagnostic
# oracle: independent-derivation
# scenario: diag-boot-pass
# profile: intv-2609-ntsc-family-p1
# program: fixtures/FX-diag-cpu-1/diag-cpu-1.listing.txt (ivs-diag-cpu-1), entered through
#   fixtures/FX-boot-stub/boot-stub.listing.txt (contract 1: J $5000 with R6 = 0340)
#
# Predicate of ivs-diag-cpu-1 (D-11, D-27, D-31, A-01): the guest state at the stop, derived by
# hand from 16-bit arithmetic and the printed tables of the GI CP-1600 Microprocessor Users Manual
# (SRC-gi-cp1600-um-1975, "pdf N" = page of the scan), before the program was ever run on the
# emulator. It was not generated by, read back from or edited to match the core. The author of
# this derivation also wrote the core, so both can share one misreading of the manual; the claim
# that rests on this file (CL-0008) stays provisional for that reason.
# Excluded on purpose: R7 and the flags at the stop (they depend on the HLT and interruptibility
# policies, C40 C45 C56), machine time (regression data only, see the cycle sum below), and the
# stack word [0340] (a project convention, C68).
#
# derivation: rules used. 16-bit two's complement words; C = carry out of bit 15, OV = signed
#   overflow, Z = result zero, S = bit 15 of the result (OPERATION OF STATUS BITS, S3.1.2, pdf 45).
# derivation: status written per instruction (STATUS CHANGE column): ADDR, ADDI: S Z C OV (pdf 53,
#   pdf 57); INCR, DECR, CLRR, MOVR, TSTR: S Z only (pdf 53); MVII, MVO, MVI@, PSHR, PULR, GSWD,
#   HLT: none (pdf 55, pdf 56, pdf 57).
# derivation: GSWD layout (control table, pdf 56): bits 0-3 and 8-11 = 0; C in bits 4 and 12, OV
#   in 5 and 13, Z in 6 and 14, S in 7 and 15. So S alone = 8080, S+OV = A0A0, C+OV+Z = 7070.
# derivation: S1. MVII #$03FF,R0: R0 = 03FF. Five ADDR R0,R0 double it: 07FE, 0FFC, 1FF8, 3FF0,
#   7FE0 (no carry out, no sign change). ADDI #$001F: 7FE0 + 001F = 7FFF, S=0 Z=0 C=0 OV=0.
#   MVO R0,$0200: [0200] = 7FFF.
# derivation: INCR R0: 7FFF + 1 = 8000; S=1, Z=0; C and OV are not written and keep 0 from the
#   ADDI. GSWD R1: S only, R1 = 8080; MVO: [0201] = 8080.
# derivation: DECR R0: 8000 - 1 = 7FFF (S=0 Z=0; C, OV keep 0). ADDI #$0001: 7FFF + 0001 = 8000;
#   no carry out of bit 15, so C=0; two positive operands give a negative result, so OV=1; S=1,
#   Z=0. GSWD R2: S and OV, R2 = A0A0; [0202] = A0A0.
# derivation: ADDR R0,R0: 8000 + 8000 = 1_0000, so R0 = 0000, C=1 (carry out), OV=1 (two negative
#   operands give a non-negative result), Z=1, S=0. GSWD R3: C, OV and Z, R3 = 7070;
#   [0203] = 7070. INCR writes S and Z only while ADD writes all four, which the three words
#   8080, A0A0 and 7070 separate.
# derivation: S2. MVII #$000A,R2; CLRR R3 (R3 = 0). Loop ADDR R2,R3; DECR R2; BNZE back (branch
#   table, pdf 54: backward displacement 0003 from PC + 2 = 5020 is 5020 + ~0003 = 501C). R2 takes
#   10, 9, ..., 1, so R3 = 10 + 9 + ... + 1 = 10 x 11 / 2 = 55 = 0037; the loop leaves when DECR
#   gives R2 = 0000 (Z=1). MVO R3,$0204: [0204] = 0037.
# derivation: S3. Stack direction (C17, resolved-primary; S3.4.6 pdf 62, S3.2.1 pdf 46, Fig 3.2.4
#   pdf 48): a push stores at R6 and then increments R6, a pull decrements R6 and then reads.
#   With R6 = 0340 from the stub: PSHR R3 writes [0340] = 0037 and R6 = 0341; PULR R5 makes
#   R6 = 0340 and R5 = [0340] = 0037. MVO R5,$0205: [0205] = 0037. MOVR R6,R0: R0 = 0340;
#   MVO R0,$0206: [0206] = 0340.
# derivation: S4. MVII #$0200,R4. MVI@ R4,R0 (indirect table, pdf 55: MMM = 4 reads at R4, then
#   R4 is post-incremented): R0 = [0200] = 7FFF, R4 = 0201. MVO R4,$0207: [0207] = 0201;
#   MVO R0,$0208: [0208] = 7FFF.
# derivation: End. MVII #$03C5,R0: R0 = 03C5; MVO R0,$020F: [020F] = 03C5, the last store.
#   HLT sets the stop latch and the CPU stops after the next instruction (control table, pdf 56);
#   TSTR R0 writes S and Z only. No later instruction writes a register or memory word.
# derivation: R1 = 8080, R2 = 0000 (loop end), R3 = 0037 (stored before the stack round trip and
#   not written after it), R4 = 0201, R5 = 0037, R6 = 0340; system RAM 0200-035F is 16 bits wide
#   (data/memmap literal row R 0200-035F w=16), so every stored word reads back whole.
# derivation: cycle hand-sum (regression information, not asserted): printed counts of pdf 53-57,
#   branch 9 taken / 7 not taken (POL-BR-01, C29), no wait states in this profile. Reset entry 5
#   (inference from the initialization timing figure, pdf 27); stub B 9 + DIS 4 + MVII 8 + J 12 =
#   33; S1 = MVII 8 + 5 x ADDR 6 + ADDI 8 + MVO 11 + INCR 6 + GSWD 6 + MVO 11 + DECR 6 + ADDI 8 +
#   GSWD 6 + MVO 11 + ADDR 6 + GSWD 6 + MVO 11 = 134; loop setup MVII 8 + CLRR 6 = 14; loop
#   10 x (ADDR 6 + DECR 6) + 9 x 9 + 7 = 208; rest = MVO 11 + PSHR 9 + PULR 11 + MVO 11 + MOVR 6 +
#   MVO 11 + MVII 8 + MVI@ 8 + MVO 11 + MVO 11 + MVII 8 + MVO 11 + HLT 4 + TSTR 6 = 126. Total
#   5 + 33 + 134 + 14 + 208 + 126 = 520 CPU cycles to the entry into the halted state.
#
# Body: one assert per clause in the runner's scenario grammar (plan 04-17): hex upper case, exact
# compare, evaluated at the stop. tests/CMakeLists.txt turns these lines into the C include that
# run.diag.predicate reads.
assert halted == yes
assert reg R0 == 03C5
assert reg R1 == 8080
assert reg R2 == 0000
assert reg R3 == 0037
assert reg R4 == 0201
assert reg R5 == 0037
assert reg R6 == 0340
assert mem 0200 == 7FFF
assert mem 0201 == 8080
assert mem 0202 == A0A0
assert mem 0203 == 7070
assert mem 0204 == 0037
assert mem 0205 == 0037
assert mem 0206 == 0340
assert mem 0207 == 0201
assert mem 0208 == 7FFF
assert mem 020F == 03C5
